1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,954
Episode Fifty-Four showed the garbage collector reclaiming heap objects.

2
00:00:04,954 --> 00:00:08,325
But how does bytecode actually run at full speed?

3
00:00:08,325 --> 00:00:12,866
The JVM starts with an interpreter — executing opcodes one by one.

4
00:00:12,866 --> 00:00:17,544
Hot methods get compiled to native machine code by the JIT compiler.

5
00:00:17,544 --> 00:00:22,360
C1 compiles fast with basic optimizations — C2 compiles deep and slow.

6
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:26,626
Today — JIT compilation, tiered execution, and deoptimization.

7
00:00:26,626 --> 00:00:28,677
Episode Fifty-Five.

8
00:00:28,677 --> 00:00:30,404
JIT Compilation.

9
00:00:30,404 --> 00:00:34,803
The interpreter executes bytecode without ahead-of-time compilation.

10
00:00:34,803 --> 00:00:39,138
Every method starts in interpreted mode — simple, portable, slower.

11
00:00:39,138 --> 00:00:43,732
The JVM profiles execution — counting loop iterations and method calls.

12
00:00:43,732 --> 00:00:47,484
Profiling data feeds the JIT — identifying hot code paths.

13
00:00:47,484 --> 00:00:51,431
Cold code stays interpreted — no compilation overhead wasted.

14
00:00:51,431 --> 00:00:55,830
Interpretation is the safety net when compiled code becomes invalid.

15
00:00:55,830 --> 00:00:58,946
Tiered compilation uses multiple JIT compilers.

16
00:00:58,946 --> 00:01:03,653
C1 — client compiler — fast compile, basic optimizations, quick warmup.

17
00:01:03,653 --> 00:01:08,360
C2 — server compiler — slow compile, aggressive inlining and loop opts.

18
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:13,001
Default on modern JDK — methods escalate from interpreted to C1 to C2.

19
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:17,642
Compilation happens on background threads — application keeps running.

20
00:01:17,642 --> 00:01:22,216
Compiled code lives in code cache — native memory separate from heap.

21
00:01:22,216 --> 00:01:26,587
A method becomes hot when invocation or loop counters exceed thresholds.

22
00:01:26,587 --> 00:01:31,018
CompileThreshold and tiered thresholds control when compilation triggers.

23
00:01:31,018 --> 00:01:35,086
Inlining replaces method calls with the callee body — huge speedup.

24
00:01:35,086 --> 00:01:39,396
Escape analysis can stack-allocate objects that never leave the method.

25
00:01:39,396 --> 00:01:43,402
Dead code elimination and constant folding happen at compile time.

26
00:01:43,402 --> 00:01:47,834
Profile-guided optimization uses runtime data for better code generation.

27
00:01:47,834 --> 00:01:51,645
Deoptimization reverts compiled code back to the interpreter.

28
00:01:51,645 --> 00:01:55,956
Happens when assumptions break — new class loaded, uncommon trap hit.

29
00:01:55,956 --> 00:02:00,329
Uncommon traps guard speculative optimizations like monomorphic calls.

30
00:02:00,329 --> 00:02:04,390
The JVM patches call sites and re-enters interpreted mode safely.

31
00:02:04,390 --> 00:02:07,702
Recompilation may follow with updated profiling data.

32
00:02:07,702 --> 00:02:11,700
Deoptimization is normal — not a bug — it preserves correctness.

33
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:14,407
The tiered execution pipeline in order.

34
00:02:14,407 --> 00:02:17,739
Level zero — pure interpretation with profiling.

35
00:02:17,739 --> 00:02:21,280
Level one — C1 compiled without profiling overhead.

36
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:25,306
Level two and three — C1 with increasing profiling detail.

37
00:02:25,306 --> 00:02:28,361
Level four — C2 fully optimized native code.

38
00:02:28,361 --> 00:02:32,734
Understanding tiers helps interpret JIT compilation log output.

39
00:02:32,734 --> 00:02:34,182
Three common mistakes.

40
00:02:34,182 --> 00:02:38,657
One — disabling tiered compilation without measuring — rarely helps.

41
00:02:38,657 --> 00:02:43,527
Two — assuming first-run performance equals steady-state — warmup matters.

42
00:02:43,527 --> 00:02:48,396
Three — micro-benchmarking without JVM warmup — measures interpreter only.

43
00:02:48,396 --> 00:02:53,595
Also — printing deoptimization events in production without understanding them.

44
00:02:53,595 --> 00:02:58,136
Benchmark with warmup iterations — let the JIT compile before timing.

45
00:02:58,136 --> 00:03:01,414
Interview question — how does the JVM JIT work?

46
00:03:01,414 --> 00:03:05,460
Bytecode starts interpreted — profiler counts hot methods.

47
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:09,924
C1 fast compile with basic opts — C2 deep optimize for hot code.

48
00:03:09,924 --> 00:03:14,248
Inlining, escape analysis, and loop unrolling at compile time.

49
00:03:14,248 --> 00:03:18,503
Deoptimization when assumptions fail — revert to interpreter.

50
00:03:18,503 --> 00:03:23,106
Warmup matters — steady-state performance differs from cold start.

51
00:03:23,106 --> 00:03:28,666
GC and JIT both shape runtime performance — collectors differ wildly.

52
00:03:28,666 --> 00:03:31,406
Episode Fifty-Six — GC Collectors.

53
00:03:31,406 --> 00:03:35,596
Serial, Parallel, G1, ZGC — and when to choose each.

54
00:03:35,596 --> 00:03:36,724
See you there.
